<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="93af5745">&lt;p&gt;
    Typically, there should by three sections in the report:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;1.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Current progress&lt;/strong&gt; of realizing the intended benefits and &lt;strong&gt;actions still
    outstanding&lt;/strong&gt;: Typical headings of this section include:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Executive summary
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Overview of program progress
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Financial benefits - for each benefit, a view of performance for the current period, last period, comparison with
        baseline and forecast.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Non-financial benefits - for each benefit, a view of performance for the current period, last period, comparison
        with baseline and forecast.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Monthly/quarterly/annual comparisons
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Recommendations
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;2.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Lessons learned&lt;/strong&gt;: This section documents the reasons why a particular benefit was
    achieved or missed the target. It enables the operations team to adjust their measures or instate corrective actions to
    drive the benefits towards their realization. A summary of the lessons learned may also benefit future investments.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;3.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Further potential benefits&lt;/strong&gt;: This section captures additional ideas from the team
    which identify other projects/initiatives that should be started to drive the benefits. Examples of such projects are
    typically targeted improvements of the process or data quality enhancement.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="2883d1df"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="61e9edbf">&lt;p&gt;
    Benefits Reports may be collated on a regular basis such as monthly or quarterly or they may be compiled to support
    formal benefit reviews with senior stakeholders.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The report should be designed in conjunction with the key stakeholders who will be receiving and using the information
    it presents. This could be done through a design workshop or an iterative process of reviewing different templates.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="ea377313"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="756f6e71">&lt;p&gt;
    To demonstrate whether a program is delivering the benefits that were outlined in the business case by
    communicating to stakeholders the progress in realizing the intended benefits of a program or project. It is also a
    valuable tool in supporting senior stakeholder engagement activities and in escalating key issues for management
    attention.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="c8b58784"></attribute>
